{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-integration", "file": "references/demo-chain-pitfall.md", "content": "# Demo-chain pitfall: integrate before spending again

A run of demonstrations can be costly without improving the prediction if each new pair is accepted and another request follows without analysis. After each response, record its dimensions, structural lattice/period, output shape, and exact correspondence; then test candidate mappings against all examples received so far. Dissimilar-looking examples may still reveal a common abstraction, so do not treat unfamiliar appearance as a reason to request yet another example.

Before each request, state internally at least two competing rules and the concrete observation expected to distinguish them. If the test rule remains uncertain but the demos already impose no further useful distinction, make a validated prediction from the evidence or acknowledge uncertainty through the protocol's allowed actions; do not reflexively consume the demo cap. A wrong answer should trigger a material reconsideration of scope, output dimensions, or mapping—not a cosmetic variant.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-integration/references/demo-chain-pitfall.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-error-recovery", "file": "references/trajectory-after-error.md", "content": "# Revising sparse trajectory hypotheses after a wrong output

A wrong submission that extends only one apparent ray is not repaired by shifting its start or changing a single cell. Re-derive the generator from each demo by recording every seed marker, color, direction vector, and endpoint. In the example reviewed here, one demo was identity (a sparse object did not trigger extension), while the larger demo showed simultaneous rays: a 7-ray extended down-right from its marker, and 6-ray(s) extended along the other diagonal direction from multiple marker locations. The test had several 9 markers plus paired 7 and 6 markers; assuming the salient 9-marker cluster was irrelevant and extending just one 6 ray / one short 7 ray was unsupported and was rejected. General lesson: compare the full input geometry to positive and negative demos; infer whether multiple marker colors or every compatible seed launches a ray, and project each ray through the full grid to the evidenced boundary. Preserve original objects and background. Do not submit a one-color or one-ray interpretation unless it accounts for all trajectory behavior in the demonstrations.

This example is not a universal rule for launching rays from all markers: use it to formulate competing hypotheses, then select the one that explains the complete demos and test configuration.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-error-recovery/references/trajectory-after-error.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving-continual", "file": "references/motif-rule-validation.md", "content": "# Motif and propagation rule validation

When demonstrations contain a salient colored motif embedded in a noisy background, do not infer repetition or ray extension from appearance alone. Maintain a coordinate ledger of every changed and unchanged cell across all demos. For a tiling/template candidate, specify the period, phase, reflection/rotation behavior, and treatment of non-background cells; for a propagation candidate, specify each seed, direction vector, stopping rule, and overwrite behavior. Replay the full generator against every demo before applying it to the test.

After an incorrect submission, reject the whole generator. A different phase, period, or orientation is not a meaningful revision unless the complete demos support it. If no candidate survives the ledger, use a demo only for a concrete distinction; do not burn attempts on plausible-looking grids. Maintain the strict action-only protocol throughout.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving-continual/references/motif-rule-validation.md"}
